>> title: Meatballs
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: beef, pork, veal, tomato, italian, braise
>> counters: Pizzeria, The Bowl Shop
>> aka: polpette, italian meatballs, meatballs in sauce, spaghetti and meatballs, meatball, meatball parm
>> pairs-with: marinara-sauce, fresh-egg-pasta
>> servings: 6
>> time: 1 hr 45 min
>> slack: unforgiving — they finish in the sauce and they finish cooked through, not pink at the centre, whatever the outside looks like
>> step.1: soak the bread to a paste
>> step.2: mix by hand, and stop early
>> step.3: roll 18 balls, chill 20 min
>> step.4: brown all over, 8 min
>> step.5: simmer 45 min in the sauce

Soak @stale white bread{3%slices}(crusts off, torn small) in @whole milk{3/4%cup}(180 mL) ~soak{10%min}, then mash it to a smooth paste with a fork.

Mix @ground beef{1%lb}(450 g; 80/20), @ground pork{1/2%lb}(225 g), @ground veal{1/2%lb}(225 g), @grated pecorino{3/4%cup}(75 g), @eggs{2%large}(100 g), @garlic{4%cloves}(minced), @flat-leaf parsley{1/4%cup}(chopped), @kosher salt{2%tsp} and @black pepper{1%tsp} into @&(~1)panade{} with your hands, and stop the moment it comes together.

Roll @&(~1)meatball mix{} into 18 balls the size of a golf ball, wetting your palms between, and ~chill{20%min} on a tray.

Brown @&(~1)meatballs{} in @extra-virgin olive oil{1/4%cup}(60 mL) in a wide #pan{} over medium-high heat ~brown{8%min}, turning them onto three or four faces rather than rolling them about.

Simmer @&(~1)browned meatballs{} in @marinara sauce{5%cup}(1.2 L; from the recipe on this shelf) ~simmer{45%min}, half covered, until a spoon goes through one without resistance.

Fry a teaspoon of the mix flat in the pan and taste it before you roll the rest, because raw meat cannot be tasted and pecorino is saltier than anyone expects. The three meats are not a flourish: beef alone goes dry, pork carries the fat, veal is what makes the crumb tender. Working the mix past the point where it holds together is what turns a meatball rubbery, which is why the instruction is to stop early rather than to mix well. They can be baked at 425°F for fifteen minutes instead of browned, which is easier and less good — the crust is paler and less of it dissolves into the sauce.
